---
/* ============================================================
   echoes-design.astro   (PAGE — src/pages/portfolio/)
   URL: /portfolio/echoes-design

   UX CASE STUDY — custom standalone page (NOT ProjectPage).
   Uses BaseLayout for nav/head/footer; all content sits inside
   one width-constrained .echoes container so margins match the
   rest of the site.

   ORDER (per Paulina's spec):
    1  logo + title + subtitle
    2  header image
    3  keywords
    4  project description
    5  video
    6  THE PROBLEM   (heading left | purple rule | body right, vertically centered)
    7  centered paragraph
    8  conditions image (echoes-2)
    9  THE APPROACH   (same two-column format) + centered paragraph under
    10 player+phone image (echoes-3)
    11 THE FINDINGS   (same two-column format)
    12 centered paragraph (findings detail + research link)
    13 VR players image (echoes-4)

   Copy proofread from Paulina's message.
   ============================================================ */

import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Echoes of Abandonment — UX Case Study — Paulina Cywoniuk"
  description="A UX case study: marking interactable objects in a narrative VR experience without breaking immersion."
>
  <article class="echoes">

    <!-- 1. LOCKUP: logo + title + subtitle -->
    <header class="lockup">
      <img src="/portfolio/echoes/echoes-logo.svg" alt="Echoes of Abandonment logo" class="lockup-logo" />
      <div class="lockup-text">
        <h1 class="lockup-title">Echoes of Abandonment</h1>
        <p class="lockup-subtitle">Interactive Storytelling VR Experience and UX Research Project</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- 2. HEADER IMAGE -->
    <div class="media">
      <img src="/portfolio/echoes/echoes-header.png" alt="Player in a VR headset interacting with a hand-sculpted clay bedroom" />
    </div>

    <!-- 3. KEYWORDS -->
    <div class="pill-bar">
      <span class="pill">VR</span>
      <span class="pill">Unity</span>
      <span class="pill">Solo</span>
      <span class="pill">4 months</span>
    </div>

    <!-- 4. PROJECT DESCRIPTION -->
    <div class="text-column">
      <p>
        This is a Virtual Reality interactive storytelling experience about a child who is
        dealing with their parents divorcing. By putting on VR goggles we are placed in a
        world sculpted by hand in clay to resemble a kid's bedroom. Behind the door we hear
        parents arguing, and our goal, as a child, is to mute this argument by playing with
        our toys.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each interactable toy has a sound attached to it, so that when we keep on playing we
        create a musical composition that allows us to again take control in our safe space
        over the world we experience.
      </p>
      <p>
        Toys are "protectors" or "guardians" of imaginary reality that help kids develop
        creativity and a sense of safety. This project opens up a conversation about dealing
        with trauma that can be caused by being abandoned by one or both parents, or dealing
        with parents' conflicts while growing up.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 5. VIDEO -->
    <div class="video-embed">
      <iframe
        src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/zE0qHiv2KMg"
        title="Echoes of Abandonment — gameplay"
        loading="lazy"
        allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"
        allowfullscreen
      ></iframe>
    </div>

    <!-- 6. THE PROBLEM -->
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2 class="case-heading">The Problem</h2>
      <div class="case-body">
        <p>
          Echoes of Abandonment was presented at the KABK Graduation Show in 2024, where
          hundreds of visitors were experiencing it. I noticed that
          <strong>to some players it wasn't as intuitive with what toys they could interact
          with.</strong> Watching them play became a real usability study, and it surfaced a
          problem I hadn't designed for nor did I find academic literature that could solve
          this problem for me. I've decided to cover the research gap and move with this
          project from the design piece to a research proposal in the Master's of Game
          Technology program.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 7. CENTERED PARAGRAPH -->
    <div class="text-column">
      <p>
        The interaction was designed quite straightforward: discover the toys, and through
        playing with them, quiet the argument behind the door. For a portion of players it
        wasn't intuitive which objects were interactive. They'd reach for scenery, miss the
        toys, or hesitate, unsure what the world was inviting them to do. In UX terms, the
        objects' affordances weren't reading clearly. A research question emerged: how should
        you mark an interactive object in VR without shattering the immersion the story
        depends on?
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 8. CONDITIONS IMAGE -->
    <div class="media">
      <img src="/portfolio/echoes/echoes-2.png" alt="The two marker conditions from the study: diegetic shader and non-diegetic exclamation icons" />
    </div>

    <!-- 9. THE APPROACH -->
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2 class="case-heading">The Approach</h2>
      <div class="case-body">
        <p>
          To answer the question rigorously rather than by intuition, I designed a
          <strong>between-subjects experiment</strong> with 35 participants comparing three
          ways of marking interactive objects known to games UX research:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Diegetic markers:</strong> cues that live inside the story world (a subtle brightness shader on interactive toys), so nothing about them breaks the fiction.</li>
          <li><strong>Non-diegetic markers:</strong> cues layered on top of the world (a floating exclamation-mark icon), clearer as a signal but foreign to the fiction.</li>
          <li><strong>A control group:</strong> no markers at all.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="text-column">
      <p>
        I measured narrative immersion using two academic tools — the Immersion Experience
        Questionnaire (short form) and the Narrative Engagement Scale, plus custom questions
        written for this study's specific question. Alongside the numbers, I ran observations
        of each playthrough and informal interviews, so I'd have the qualitative "why" behind
        the quantitative "what."
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 10. PLAYER + PHONE IMAGE -->
    <div class="media">
      <img src="/portfolio/echoes/echoes-3.jpg" alt="A participant playing while the in-game view is shown on a phone" />
    </div>

    <!-- 11. THE FINDINGS -->
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2 class="case-heading">The Findings</h2>
      <div class="case-body">
        <p>
          The headline result failed to reject the null hypothesis. Statistical analysis in
          SPSS (ANOVA and Kruskal-Wallis) showed no significant difference in narrative
          immersion between the three conditions with effect sizes below η² = .02. Marker
          design, on its own, did not measurably move immersion in either direction.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 12. CENTERED PARAGRAPH (findings detail + research link) -->
    <div class="text-column">
      <p>
        For a moment that read like failure. However, it was the most useful thing the study
        could have told me, once I looked at the qualitative data for why:
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>1. Non-diegetic markers were actually the clearer signifiers.</strong> In the
        open responses, participants, especially less experienced gamers, found the
        exclamation-mark icon easier to read as "you can use this." The subtle diegetic shader
        divided people: some loved its invisibility, others simply didn't notice it. This
        directly addresses my original grad-show problem: the "immersion-breaking" marker was
        the one that solved the affordance issue, and it didn't measurably cost immersion to
        do so.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>2. Sound design was the real immersion-breaker.</strong> Across every
        condition, participants pointed at the audio, not the markers, as the thing pulling
        them out of the experience. They complained about the layered sound of the musical
        toys and parent argument being too distracting to realise what the argument was about
        — despite the goal of the game being not to listen to the argument and mute it
        completely through playing music. The variable I was studying turned out to be less
        influential than a variable I wasn't. That's the study's most important design lesson:
        auditory UI deserves the same scrutiny as visual UI in VR, and I'd designed the sound
        with far less rigor than the markers.
      </p>
      <p class="case-note">
        <em>Make sure to read a summary of my study in my <a href="/research">research section</a>,
        where you can also find my full paper and publication.</em>
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 13. VR PLAYERS IMAGE -->
    <div class="media">
      <img src="/portfolio/echoes/echoes-4.jpg" alt="VR players in the Echoes of Abandonment environment" />
    </div>

    <a href="/portfolio" class="back-link">← Back to portfolio</a>

  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* ============================================================
     CONTAINER — same width rules as every other page.
     ============================================================ */
  .echoes {
    max-width: var(--max-width);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 4rem) var(--space-page);
  }

  /* ---------- 1. LOCKUP (logo left, title+subtitle right) ---------- */
  .lockup {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    margin-bottom: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);
  }

  .lockup-logo {
    width: clamp(80px, 12vw, 150px);   /* KNOB: logo size */
    height: auto;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .lockup-title {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  .lockup-subtitle {
    font-family: var(--font-subtitle);
    font-size: clamp(1rem, 2vw, 1.3rem);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
  }

  /* ---------- IMAGES (full width of container) ---------- */
  .media {
    width: 100%;
    border-radius: 16px;
    overflow: hidden;
    margin: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem) 0;
    box-shadow:
      0 4px 16px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.08),
      0 1px 4px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);
  }

  .media img { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; }

  /* ---------- KEYWORDS ---------- */
  .pill-bar {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: clamp(1rem, 3vw, 2.5rem);
    padding: clamp(0.75rem, 2vw, 1.1rem) clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 2.5rem);
    border: 1.5px solid var(--color-purple);
    border-radius: 999px;
    margin: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem) auto;
    width: fit-content;
    max-width: 100%;
  }

  .pill {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: clamp(0.9rem, 1.6vw, 1.1rem);
    color: var(--color-text);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* ---------- CENTERED TEXT COLUMNS (points 4, 7, 9-under, 12) ---------- */
  .text-column {
    max-width: 72ch;
    margin: 0 auto clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem);
    font-size: clamp(1rem, 1.7vw, 1.1rem);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .text-column p { margin-bottom: 1.1rem; }
  .text-column p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  .case-note { margin-top: 1.5rem !important; color: var(--color-text-muted); }
  .case-note a { color: var(--color-purple); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-purple); }

  /* ---------- VIDEO ---------- */
  .video-embed {
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    border-radius: 16px;
    overflow: hidden;
    margin: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem) 0;
    box-shadow:
      0 4px 16px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.08),
      0 1px 4px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);
  }

  .video-embed iframe { width: 100%; height: 100%; border: none; display: block; }

  /* ============================================================
     CASE SECTIONS (6, 9, 11) — heading LEFT | purple rule | body RIGHT
     Heading is VERTICALLY CENTERED against the body (align-items: center).
     ============================================================ */
  .case-section {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.4fr;   /* KNOB: heading vs body width balance */
    gap: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    align-items: center;                 /* heading centered to middle of body */
    margin: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem) auto;
    max-width: 950px;
  }

  .case-heading {
    font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 3rem);   /* KNOB */
    color: var(--color-text);
    line-height: 1.1;
    text-align: left;
  }

  .case-body {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-purple);   /* THE PURPLE DIVIDER. KNOB */
    padding-left: clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 2rem);
    font-size: clamp(1rem, 1.6vw, 1.1rem);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .case-body p { margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .case-body p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  .case-body ul { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0.75rem 0 0; }
  .case-body li { position: relative; padding-left: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
  .case-body li::before {
    content: "•"; position: absolute; left: 0;
    color: var(--color-purple); font-weight: bold;
  }

  /* ---------- BACK LINK ---------- */
  .back-link {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-top: 2rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
    color: var(--color-blue);
  }

  /* ============================================================
     MOBILE — lockup and case sections stack vertically.
     ============================================================ */
    @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .lockup {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
      grid-template-areas:
        "logo     title"
        "subtitle subtitle";
      align-items: center;
      column-gap: 0.75rem;
      row-gap: 0.5rem;
    }
    .lockup-text     { display: contents; }
    .lockup-logo     { grid-area: logo; width: 3.5rem; }
    .lockup-title    { grid-area: title; }
    .lockup-subtitle { grid-area: subtitle; }

    .case-section { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 1rem; align-items: start; }
  }
</style>